meta {
  name: set-accepting-orders
  type: http
  seq: 3
}

patch {
  url: {{base_url}}/api/admin/v1/settings/accepting-orders
  body: json
  auth: bearer
}

headers {
  Content-Type: application/json
}

auth:bearer {
  token: {{access_token}}
}

body:json {
  {
    "accepting_orders": true
  }
}

tests {
  test("responds 200", function () {
    expect(res.status).to.equal(200)
  })

  test("reports the switch back", function () {
    expect(res.body.data.accepting_orders).to.be.true
  })

  test("and did not disturb the rest of the settings", function () {
    // The whole reason this is its own route rather than a field on PATCH /settings: closing up
    // mid-service must not carry along a stale tax rate from a form somebody left open.
    expect(res.body.data.tax_bps).to.be.a("number")
    expect(res.body.data.slug).to.be.a("string")
  })
}

docs {
  # Open / close for orders

The "we are open" switch staff flip at the start and end of service ([D18](../../docs/DECISIONS.md)).

**Not manager-gated**, unlike everything else under `/settings`. Closing up is a floor action taken
by whoever is actually standing there, and routing it through a manager would mean orders keep
arriving after the kitchen has gone home. The same argument already leaves menu availability open
to every role.

Distinct from `status` (active / inactive / archived), which is the restaurant's lifecycle on the
platform. Closing for the night must not archive the restaurant, orphan its order history or drop
it out of the public directory -- exactly the split `menu_item` makes between `is_available` and
`status`.

Set it to `false` and then try `guest / place-order`: it answers `TX_RST_008` (409), and the diner
menu renders read-only because `accepting_orders` rides on the public restaurant summary.
}
